     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-28 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>New Electron Camera Reveals Light-Driven Quantum Jumps in Graphene</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Phys.org</span> <span>#Quantum Physics</span> <span>#Graphene</span> <span>#Electron Microscopy</span></div>
       <p>Physicists from Regensburg, Marburg, and Jülich have utilized a novel slow-motion electron camera to observe a seemingly paradoxical phenomenon in graphene: how intense electric fields drive electrons to jump across energy gaps. This breakthrough, made possible by an innovative combination of laser technology and electron microscopy, sheds light on processes known as Landau-Zener-Majorana transitions, which are believed to play a significant role in high-speed semiconductor electronics. Their findings were published in the journal Nature.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-28/new-electron-camera-reveals-light-driven-quantum-jumps-in-gr.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The observed electron behavior is likened to an elevator instantly jumping from the third to the twentieth floor without traversing the intermediate levels—a feat possible within the quantum world. In semiconductors, electrons typically remain confined to specific energy bands separated by a forbidden gap. However, during these Landau-Zener-Majorana transitions, electrons appear to overcome this forbidden zone with remarkable ease. The research team, comprising experts from the University of Regensburg, Philipps-Universität Marburg, and Forschungszentrum Jülich, successfully visualized these ultrafast dynamics within graphene.</p><p>To capture these extremely rapid processes, the team developed a unique apparatus that marries laser technology with electron microscopy. Vincent Eggers, the study's first author, explained the necessity of observing &quot;as many electrons as possible at one time to catch those that undergo a quantum mechanical transition.&quot; This was achieved using a photoemission momentum microscope, which maps the energies and states of motion of electrons rather than producing magnified images. Rupert Huber noted that the team dedicated many years to this experiment, enabling them to &quot;track electrons moving across the entire energy landscape of a solid in real time.&quot;</p><p>The precision measurements relied on tailor-made, atomically thin carbon layers of the highest purity, produced at Forschungszentrum Jülich. Stefan Tautz from Jülich emphasized that such experiments demand &quot;material that is nearly perfect at the atomic level&quot; and highlighted the use of &quot;rotated&quot; graphene, a Jülich specialty, for clarifying quantum dynamics. The slow-motion observations revealed a surprising result: under the influence of strong light fields, electrons overcome the band gap with a tunneling probability of up to 100%. This high probability occurs even when energy states are clearly separated, with the speed at which electrons approach the band gap being the decisive factor, fundamentally distinguishing these dynamic transitions from standard quantum mechanical tunneling.</p><p>This new methodology holds promise for investigating not only fundamental quantum mechanical transitions but also the mechanisms that limit the targeted control of electrons. Initial measurements on graphene already indicate how interactions with the crystal lattice can disrupt the electrons' orderly movement. Such insights are crucial for developing future technologies where electron control shifts from electrical voltages to the extremely fast oscillations of light. Giacomo Inzani of the University of Regensburg summarized the achievement, stating, &quot;Our experiment opens a panoramic window into the quantum world... Our method can be applied to many other quantum materials, opening up completely new possibilities for controlling their electronic properties selectively with light.&quot;</p><p>What to watch: Further application of this method to other quantum materials for advanced electronic control.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Thoroughly details the scientific breakthrough, research team, methodology, and quotes without unsupported claims.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-motion-electron-camera-reveals-driven.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
