     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-28 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Palakkad health workers undergo stroke symptom training</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Nursing officers in Palakkad received training on stroke identification and treatment ahead of World Stroke Day. The session, led by general physician Sona N., covered ischaemic and haemorrhagic stroke types and risk factors including smoking and stress. Health institutions will utilize the 'BE FAST' acronym—Balance, Eyes, Face, Arms, Speech, and Time—to help the public recognize symptoms.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-28/palakkad-health-workers-undergo-stroke-symptom-training.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Patients must receive medical care within four-and-a-half hours of symptom onset for time-sensitive treatments. The District Medical Office and National Health Mission organized the programme, confirming that treatment is available at the Government District Hospital for those experiencing common signs like facial drooping and sudden weakness.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately summarizes the training, the condition types, and the organizational backing without exceeding the word count.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/kerala/health-workers-to-lead-stroke-awareness-drive-in-palakkad/article71635826.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
