     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-28 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Proposal for national parenting support strategy in Australia</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Despite significant annual investment in child-related policies, Australia continues to face high rates of child maltreatment, family violence, and mental health issues. Experts argue that current policy architecture focuses on reactive systems rather than proactive support for parents, who are the primary influence on child well-being.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-28/proposal-for-national-parenting-support-strategy-in-australi.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Researchers advocate for a public health approach to parenting, noting that current support is fragmented and often unavailable until a crisis occurs. A coherent national strategy could shift the focus toward prevention, addressing harms like neglect and bullying before they require intervention by statutory child protection services.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief captures the essence of the argument for a national parenting strategy and the critique of current reactive policy frameworks.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-national-parenting-strategy-australia-efforts.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
