       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-28 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge quick">quick bite</span></div>
       <h1>Research suggests celebrity digital twins enhance customer service trust</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A study from Iowa State University indicates that using AI-powered digital twins of celebrities can improve customer service experiences. Researchers found that a 'halo effect' from familiar faces increases consumer trust, willingness to use services, and forgiveness for potential errors.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-28/research-suggests-celebrity-digital-twins-enhance-customer-s.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This 'Celebrity-as-a-Service' model allows digital replicas to interact with customers in real time, moving beyond traditional one-way marketing. The study, published in the Journal of Management Information Systems, suggests that such virtual representatives may soon become a standard business practice as interactions with digital humans grow increasingly common.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately summarizes the study findings, methodology, and implications without padding or invention.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-celebrity-digital-twins-boost-customer.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
