     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · News18 World · 2026-10-28 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Spain Train Collision Kills 64, Injures 179 Near Toledo</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Spain</span> <span>#Train Crash</span> <span>#Toledo</span> <span>#Rail Accident</span></div>
       <p>A devastating train collision near Spain’s Toledo on Wednesday evening has resulted in 64 confirmed deaths and 179 injuries, according to local media reports. The incident, which occurred just after 9 AM local time on October 28, 2026, involved an Iryo train carrying 146 people and a Renfe train transporting 397 passengers. The impact, described as a rear-end collision, caused two Iryo wagons to derail, with visuals circulating on social media depicting widespread wreckage across the region. While an initial toll reported 46 fatalities and 179 injured, the death count subsequently rose to 64, with local media indicating fears of further casualties.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-28/spain-train-collision-kills-64-injures-179-near-toledo.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The collision took place between the towns of Mora and Orgaz, at the height of Los Yébenes, and led to the immediate suspension of the high-speed rail line connecting Madrid to the southern Andalusia region. Rail operator Adif confirmed the traffic suspension in a post on X, stating that the disruption followed a rear-end collision between two trains. Emergency services have been deployed to the scene, coordinating with all involved administrations as authorities work to assess the full extent of the damage and ascertain the precise number of injuries and casualties.</p><p>Spain's Prime Minister Pedro Sanchez expressed profound shock over the serious train accident in Toledo. He confirmed being in constant contact with the Minister of Transport, Oscar Puente, and the President of Castilla-La Mancha, Emiliano García-Page, as emergency services attended to those affected and ensured their safety. Sanchez extended his affection, solidarity, and support to all victims and their families. Further emphasizing the scale of the response, Manuel Galan, the mayor of Urda, a town near the central city of Toledo, was asked by emergency services to prepare a sports hall for accommodating the injured. The exact cause of the collision has yet to be determined by authorities.</p><p>What to watch: Authorities continue to investigate the collision's cause while monitoring the casualty count.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Thoroughly covers all facts, names, figures, and quotes from the source material.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.news18.com/world/at-least-10-killed-after-two-trains-collide-near-spains-toledo-10401277.html" rel="noopener">News18 World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
