       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-10-28 · editor 10/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Wedding Cash Gifts: ITAT Ruling Clarifies Tax Treatment of Bank Deposits</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Income Tax</span> <span>#ITAT</span> <span>#Cash Deposits</span> <span>#Wedding Gifts</span></div>
       <p>A recent ruling by the Income Tax Appellate Tribunal (ITAT) in Pune has provided significant clarity for taxpayers regarding the treatment of cash deposits in bank accounts, particularly those attributed to wedding gifts. In the case of Smt. Manisha Madhukar Shatalwar vs Income Tax Officer, Ward-3(2), Nagpur, for assessment year 2016-17, the tribunal accepted ₹2.75 lakh of a bank employee’s ₹3 lakh cash deposits as explained by wedding gifts and accumulated savings. However, it sustained an addition of ₹25,000 to her taxable income due to the absence of supporting confirmations for some of the gifts. Accountant Member Manish Borad pronounced this order on October 14, 2026.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-28/wedding-cash-gifts-itat-ruling-clarifies-tax-treatment-of-ba.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The taxpayer, employed with Indian Overseas Bank, had declared an income of ₹3,10,270 in her income tax return filed on August 1, 2016. Her marriage took place on December 3, 2014, and she informed the tribunal that she had received cash gifts totaling ₹3,19,422 from relatives, family members, and friends on this occasion. Subsequently, she deposited ₹3 lakh of this money into her bank account in six instalments of ₹50,000 each to create a fixed deposit. Initially, both the assessing officer and the first appellate authority had treated the entire ₹3 lakh as unexplained cash deposits, prompting her appeal to the ITAT.</p><p>Before the ITAT, the taxpayer submitted a list of individuals who had given her wedding gifts. The tribunal acknowledged that receiving cash gifts at weddings is a customary practice in India and noted that the relevant statutory provision does not prescribe a monetary ceiling for such receipts. The ITAT considered several factors in its decision to accept the majority of the deposit, including her employment with a bank, her history of regular income tax return filings, the provided donor list, and her explanation that the funds were used for a fixed deposit. Her accumulated savings were also taken into account.</p><p>The tribunal's partial relief meant that ₹2.75 lakh was treated as explained. The remaining ₹25,000, however, remained taxable as unexplained money. This was specifically because the taxpayer failed to furnish confirmations for cash gifts that individually exceeded ₹10,000. This aspect of the ruling underscores a crucial lesson for ordinary savers and taxpayers: while wedding gifts are often accepted, their genuineness must be supported by evidence if challenged by tax authorities.</p><p>For taxpayers, the practical takeaway is the importance of meticulously preserving details of wedding gifts, including the names of donors and the amounts received. These records should be maintained alongside bank statements showing when the money was deposited or invested. This documentation can be vital in establishing a clear link between the claimed receipt and the funds in the account if the source of a deposit is ever questioned. It is important to remember that this specific decision concerned assessment year 2016-17 and was made under the Income Tax Act, 1961 provisions applicable to that year, and thus should not be interpreted as a blanket exemption for all cash deposits or a general rule that all marriage gifts will be accepted without scrutiny.</p><p>What to watch: Future ITAT rulings on cash gift documentation.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive and accurate summary of the ITAT ruling, including the specific case details and the practical lessons for taxpayers provided in the source.</p>
